<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Hubricon — Margin Audit — {{ client.company_name or client.contact_email }}</title>
<style>
:root{
  --bg: #050A1F; --bg-raise: #0A1130; --amber: #FFC000;
  --ink-100: rgba(244,246,252,1); --ink-70: rgba(244,246,252,.7); --ink-60: rgba(244,246,252,.6);
  --rule-20: rgba(244,246,252,.2); --rule-08: rgba(244,246,252,.08);
  --red: #E0604F; --green: #5FBF8F;
  --font-head: "Arial Narrow", "Helvetica Neue Condensed", Impact, sans-serif;
  --font-mono: ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
}
*{ box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
body{ background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink-60); font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.7; padding: 56px 24px 120px; }
h1,h2{ font-family: var(--font-head); font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink-100);
  text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .02em; line-height: 1.05; }
h1{ font-size: 40px; } h2{ font-size: 20px; margin-bottom: 6px; }
.wrap{ max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; }
.logo{ font-family: var(--font-head); font-weight: 700; font-size: 18px; color: var(--ink-100);
  text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; }
.logo b{ color: var(--amber); }
.meta{ font-size: 12px; color: var(--ink-60); margin: 6px 0 40px; }
section{ margin-bottom: 52px; }
.card{ background: var(--bg-raise); border: 1px solid var(--rule-08); border-radius: 16px; padding: 22px 24px; }
.kpis{ display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 12px; margin: 20px 0; }
.kpi{ background: var(--bg-raise); border: 1px solid var(--rule-08); border-radius: 14px; padding: 14px 16px; }
.kpi b{ display: block; font-family: var(--font-head); font-size: 24px; color: var(--ink-100); }
.kpi span{ font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; }
ol.actions{ list-style: none; counter-reset: n; }
ol.actions li{ counter-increment: n; display: flex; gap: 14px; padding: 14px 0;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-08); color: var(--ink-70); }
ol.actions li::before{ content: counter(n, decimal-leading-zero); font-family: var(--font-head);
  font-size: 20px; color: var(--amber); }
.tag{ font-size: 10px; letter-spacing: .08em; color: var(--amber); display: block; }
img.chart{ max-width: 100%; border-radius: 12px; margin: 14px 0; display: block; }
.narrate{ border-left: 2px solid var(--amber); padding: 4px 0 4px 14px; font-size: 13px;
  color: var(--ink-70); margin-top: 12px; }
table{ width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 12.5px; margin-top: 12px; }
th{ text-align: left; color: var(--ink-70); font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase;
  font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: .06em; padding: 6px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-20); }
td{ padding: 6px 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-08); }
td.num{ text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.risk{ color: var(--red); } .good{ color: var(--green); }
.small{ font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--ink-60); }
footer{ margin-top: 60px; font-size: 11px; color: var(--ink-60); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-08); padding-top: 16px; }
@media print{ body{ padding: 20px; } }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">

<span class="logo">Hubric<b>o</b>n</span>
<h1>Margin Audit</h1>
<p class="meta">{{ client.company_name or client.contact_email }} · prepared {{ today }} · run {{ run.id[:8] }}
{%- if run.git_sha %} · engine {{ run.engine_version }} @ {{ run.git_sha }}{% endif %}</p>

<section>
  <div class="kpis">
    <div class="kpi"><b>${{ "{:,.0f}".format(totals.revenue) }}</b><span>revenue, latest period</span></div>
    <div class="kpi"><b class="{{ 'good' if totals.net >= 0 else 'risk' }}">${{ "{:,.0f}".format(totals.net) }}</b><span>true net margin</span></div>
    <div class="kpi"><b class="{{ 'risk' if totals.skus_at_risk else '' }}">{{ totals.skus_at_risk }}</b><span>SKUs at stockout risk</span></div>
    <div class="kpi"><b class="{{ 'risk' if totals.bleed > 0 else '' }}">${{ "{:,.0f}".format(totals.bleed) }}</b><span>ad bleed identified</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Moves</h2>
    {% if actions %}
    <ol class="actions">
      {% for a in actions %}
      <li><div><span class="tag">{{ a.tag }}</span>{{ a.text }}</div></li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ol>
    {% else %}
    <p>No urgent moves this run — the follow-up sections carry the detail.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</section>

{% if ledger %}
<section>
  <div class="card">
    <h2>The Profit Record</h2>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:2px">Every move we make is written on your Profit Record with the dollars expected, your decision, and the dollars measured on your own later exports — cumulative, since day one.</p>
    <div class="kpis" style="margin:14px 0 4px">
      <div class="kpi"><b class="good">${{ "{:,.0f}".format(ledger_measured) }}</b><span>proven to date</span></div>
      <div class="kpi"><b>{{ ledger|length }}</b><span>moves issued</span></div>
      <div class="kpi"><b>{{ ledger|selectattr("status", "in", ["approved", "done"])|list|length }}</b><span>approved or completed</span></div>
    </div>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Module</th><th>Move</th><th class="num">Expected</th><th>Status</th><th class="num">Measured</th></tr>
      {% for d in ledger[:12] %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ d.module|upper }}</td>
        <td>{{ d.action_text }}</td>
        <td class="num">{% if d.expected_impact_usd %}${{ "{:,.0f}".format(d.expected_impact_usd|float) }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>
        <td>{{ d.status }}</td>
        <td class="num {{ 'good' if d.measured_impact_usd and d.measured_impact_usd|float > 0 else '' }}">{% if d.measured_impact_usd is not none %}${{ "{:,.0f}".format(d.measured_impact_usd|float) }}{% else %}—{% endif %}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </table>
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if chart_stockout %}
<section>
  <h2>Module 01 · Probabilistic inventory</h2>
  <p>{{ inventory|length }} SKUs simulated against supplier lead times ({{ run.params.simulations }} Monte Carlo paths each).</p>
  <img class="chart" src="data:image/png;base64,{{ chart_stockout }}" alt="Stockout risk by SKU">
  <div class="narrate">Bars past the dashed line are SKUs more likely than 1-in-4 to sell out before a
  reorder placed today could arrive. The reorder quantities in the table are sized for a
  95% service level.</div>
  <table>
    <tr><th>SKU</th><th class="num">Stockout P</th><th class="num">Days cover</th><th class="num">On hand</th><th class="num">Inbound</th><th class="num">Reorder point</th><th class="num">Order qty</th></tr>
    {% for r in inventory[:15] %}
    <tr>
      <td>{{ r.sku }}</td>
      <td class="num {{ 'risk' if r.stockout_probability and r.stockout_probability|float >= 0.25 else '' }}">{{ "%.0f%%"|format(r.stockout_probability|float * 100) }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.days_of_cover }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.on_hand_units }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.inbound_units }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.reorder_point }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.reorder_qty }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if chart_elasticity or elasticity %}
<section>
  <h2>Module 02 · Price elasticity</h2>
  {% if chart_elasticity %}
  <img class="chart" src="data:image/png;base64,{{ chart_elasticity }}" alt="Price vs demand">
  <div class="narrate">ε below −1 means demand is elastic — price cuts grow revenue. Between −1 and 0 the
  product is price-insensitive: a careful increase converts almost directly into margin.</div>
  {% endif %}
  {% set scored = elasticity|selectattr("status", "equalto", "ok")|selectattr("level", "equalto", "sku")|list %}
  {% if scored %}
  <table>
    <tr><th>SKU</th><th class="num">&epsilon; used</th><th class="num">95% range</th>
        <th class="num">&epsilon; from this SKU alone</th><th class="num">Own weight</th>
        <th class="num">R&sup2;</th><th class="num">Periods</th></tr>
    {% for r in scored[:12] %}
    <tr>
      <td>{{ r.item_id }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.elasticity }}</td>
      <td class="num">{% if r.details and r.details.ci95 %}[{{ r.details.ci95[0] }}, {{ r.details.ci95[1] }}]{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="num">{% if r.details and r.details.epsilon_raw is not none %}{{ r.details.epsilon_raw }}{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="num">{% if r.details and r.details.shrinkage_weight is not none %}{{ "%.0f%%"|format(r.details.shrinkage_weight|float * 100) }}{% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.r_squared }}</td>
      <td class="num">{{ r.n_periods }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
  <p class="small">Two elasticities per SKU, because one of them would be a guess dressed as a
  measurement. &ldquo;&epsilon; from this SKU alone&rdquo; is what its own price history says;
  &ldquo;&epsilon; used&rdquo; pulls that toward the rest of your catalog by exactly as much as
  its own history is uncertain, and &ldquo;own weight&rdquo; is how much of its own answer it kept.
  A SKU with five months and a price that barely moved keeps little; one with three years keeps
  nearly all of it. The range is a Student-t interval on heteroskedasticity-robust
  (HC3) standard errors &mdash; at five periods the right critical value is 3.18, not 1.96,
  and using 1.96 would print a range 38% narrower than the data supports.</p>
  {% endif %}
  {% set skipped = elasticity|rejectattr("status", "equalto", "ok")|list %}
  {% if skipped %}
  <p class="small">{{ skipped|length }} item(s) not scored &mdash;
  {{ skipped|selectattr("status", "equalto", "insufficient_price_variation")|list|length }} had effectively one price all year
  (that itself is a finding: no price testing has happened), the rest lacked enough monthly history.</p>
  {% endif %}
  <p class="small"><b>What this number is, and what it is not.</b> &epsilon; is fitted from the
  prices you set and the volumes that followed. Those prices were chosen by you, often in
  response to how demand was running &mdash; and when that happens the fit reads demand as
  <i>less</i> price-sensitive than it is. On simulated sellers who reprice off last month's
  numbers, the error is about 0.4 of an &epsilon; in that direction, which is the direction that
  argues for raising prices. It is not corrected, because correcting it needs a price change made
  for a reason unrelated to demand. Every step on this report is exactly that: the moves we make
  become the clean history the next fit is built on.</p>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if price_tests %}
<section>
  <h2>Price-testing program</h2>
  <p>Deliberate, tracked price moves &mdash; Buy Box share watched so suppression is caught in days, and every completed test feeds the next elasticity fit. The size of each step is not a house rule: it is solved for against how much your own history actually pins down, inside the &plusmn;5% a cycle your terms authorise.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>SKU</th><th class="num">Move</th><th>Window</th><th class="num">Buy Box before → during</th><th>Status</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
    {% for t in price_tests %}
    <tr>
      <td>{{ t.sku }}</td>
      <td class="num">${{ "%.2f"|format(t.baseline_price|float) }} → ${{ "%.2f"|format(t.test_price|float) }}</td>
      <td>{{ t.start_date or "—" }} .. {{ t.end_date or "—" }}</td>
      <td class="num {{ 'risk' if t.buy_box_share_before and t.buy_box_share_during and t.buy_box_share_before|float - t.buy_box_share_during|float >= 10 else '' }}">
        {{ t.buy_box_share_before or "—" }}% → {{ t.buy_box_share_during or "—" }}%</td>
      <td>{{ t.status }}</td>
      <td class="small">{{ t.outcome_notes or "" }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if chart_ads or ads %}
<section>
  <h2>Module 03 · Bayesian ad efficiency</h2>
  {% if chart_ads %}
  <img class="chart" src="data:image/png;base64,{{ chart_ads }}" alt="Ad saturation curves">
  <div class="narrate">The curve is the fitted response of attributed sales to spend. Where the amber line
  (current spend) sits right of the red line (marginal break-even), the last dollars in are
  buying less than a dollar back.</div>
  {% endif %}
  {% for r in ads if r.bleed_terms %}
  {% if loop.first %}
  <table>
    <tr><th>Campaign</th><th>Search term</th><th class="num">Spend, $0 sales</th><th class="num">Clicks</th></tr>
  {% endif %}
    {% for t in r.bleed_terms[:5] %}
    <tr><td>{{ r.campaign_name }}</td><td>{{ t.search_term }}</td><td class="num risk">${{ t.spend }}</td><td class="num">{{ t.clicks }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
  {% if loop.last %}</table>{% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if chart_margin %}
<section>
  <h2>Module 04 · True net margin</h2>
  <img class="chart" src="data:image/png;base64,{{ chart_margin }}" alt="Margin decomposition">
  <div class="narrate">Revenue split into {{ fee_label }}, landed COGS, allocated ad spend, and what's actually
  left. Ad allocation is proportional to revenue share within the period.</div>
</section>
{% endif %}

<footer>
  Hubricon &middot; Managed Profit &middot; methodology: Monte Carlo lead-time demand (lognormal lead
  times, lognormal demand rates sharing a common factor across SKUs, Poisson counts); log-log OLS
  elasticity with traffic controls where available, HC3 standard errors, Student-t intervals, and
  each SKU's estimate shrunk toward your catalog by the ratio of its own sampling variance to the
  spread between your SKUs; price steps sized against the 5th-percentile outcome rather than the
  average one; Hill saturation fits for ad response; fee-change alerts false-discovery controlled
  across the whole sweep, so the number of tests behind an alert is priced in rather than ignored.
  Every published percentile carries the simulation error behind it. Items without enough data are
  reported as such, never estimated. Derivations and limits: engine/MATH_METHODS.md.
</footer>

</div>
</body>
</html>
